The VI Program Manager (VIPM) is responsible for reviewing and approving VI production requests by ensuring the production is appropriate, avoids duplication, and will reach the widest possible audience. The VIPM must:

- Complete Section II of the DD Form 1995;
- Approve the production request after verifying the requirement is adequately defined, essential, properly validated, and all prerequisites are met;
- Calculate and enter the projected cost of the production;
- Assign a PIN (for Defense inventory productions) or PAN (for local-use-only productions);
- Complete and forward a current review report to DIMOC, Tobyhanna;
- Manage all DD Form 1995s submitted and maintain records of completed Marine Corps PIN and PAN productions;
- Serve as the liaison between DMA and the Office of Primary Responsibility (OPR);
- Forward a digital master and completed production folder (including legal and public release clearance) to DIMOC within five calendar days of receipt from COMCAM production activities.

According to Mattis, operating under continuing resolutions for 10 of the last 11 years has created significant operational risks, including:  
- Inability to provide timely and predictable funding for ongoing operations, requiring 15-day congressional notifications for each funding increment;  
- Failure to pay troops by the end of the fiscal year;  
- Inability to recruit required numbers of soldiers and airmen to fill critical manning shortfalls;  
- Inability to maintain ships at sea with proper balance between operations and maintenance;  
- Grounding of aircraft due to lack of maintenance and spare parts;  
- Depletion of ammunition, training, and manpower needed to deter war;  
- Delays in vital acquisition programs necessary for modernization;  
- Negative impact on troop morale due to persistent budget uncertainty.  

These conditions undermine military readiness, modernization, and the ability to deter conflict, as highlighted by Mattis in his testimony before the House Armed Services Committee.

The Air Force Discharge Review Board (AFDRB) is implementing several procedural reforms to improve fairness in cases involving PTSD, TBI, or sexual trauma, as part of a settlement in Johnson v. Kendall. These reforms include:  
- Establishing a one-year pilot program allowing veterans who lack sufficient evidence of a mental health condition or sexual trauma to supplement their records;  
- Requiring that all medical opinions be provided in writing;  
- Providing a dedicated phone number for applicants to ask questions;  
- Training AFDRB members and staff on how mental health conditions can contribute to misconduct and on identifying and addressing unconscious bias against individuals with disabilities or trauma histories;  
- Offering a universal video teleconference option for veterans who wish to appear personally but cannot travel to Washington, D.C.;  
- Advising applicants of their right to request accommodations, including for disabilities.  

These reforms apply to future discharge upgrade applications and are designed to ensure fair, equitable, and accessible reviews for veterans with service-connected mental health conditions or experiences of sexual trauma.

A request for a waiver to this regulation must include:  
- Justification with a full analysis of the expected benefits;  
- Formal review by the activity's senior legal officer;  
- Endorsement by the commander or senior leader of the requesting activity;  
- Submission through higher headquarters to the policy proponent (Assistant Secretary of the Army (Acquisition, Logistics and Technology)).  

These elements are mandated by the source chunk (AR 70-13_85329). Auxiliary chunks provide additional context on waiver processes in other regulations but do not alter or expand the mandatory elements specified in the source.

To verify a Soldier's participation in combat for the purpose of earning a bronze service star on the European-African-Middle Eastern Campaign Medal, the following documentation or criteria are required:

- Assignment or attachment to and presence for duty with a unit during the period it participated in combat; or  
- Being under orders in the combat zone and meeting any one of the following:  
  - Awarded a combat decoration;  
  - Furnished a certificate by a Commanding General (CG) of a corps or higher unit or independent force stating the Soldier actually participated in combat;  
  - Served at a normal post of duty (not as an inspector, observer, or visitor);  
  - Aboard a vessel in a non-passenger status, with a certificate from the home port commander confirming service in the combat zone; or  
- Being an evadee or escapee in the combat zone, or recovered from POW status in the combat zone during the campaign period (time spent in confinement or under enemy control does not count).

