The key guiding principles outlined by Lucian Niemeyer for applying the requested $10.5 billion in military construction and family housing funding are:

- Using every program and funding source available to eliminate waste in DoD infrastructure;  
- Advocating for adequate funding for installations accounts;  
- Protecting military installations from incompatible development and enhancing the combat credibility of test and training ranges;  
- Enhancing energy security for critical facilities;  
- Exploring new opportunities for third-party partnerships;  
- Working with military engineering contracting communities to develop and execute smarter contracts;  
- Ensuring the welfare of DoD personnel and resources through strong environmental stewardship and occupational safety programs;  
- Collaborating with defense communities nationwide to support military bases and families.

These principles aim to achieve measurable results by prioritizing readiness, resilience, and efficient use of resources, while also addressing excess infrastructure through facility reviews, demolition of unused buildings, and reductions in unnecessary leases.

Common Article 3 explicitly prohibits the following conduct in non-international armed conflicts:

- Violence to life and person, including murder, mutilation, cruel treatment, and torture;
- Taking of hostages;
- Outrages upon personal dignity, in particular humiliating and degrading treatment;
- The passing of sentences and carrying out of executions without a previous judgment by a regularly constituted court affording all judicial guarantees recognized as indispensable by civilized peoples.

These prohibitions apply to persons taking no active part in hostilities, such as those who have laid down their arms or are otherwise hors de combat.

To earn the 7-skill level in AFSC 4C071, the mandatory training requirements are:  
1) Completion of all core tasks marked with an asterisk (*) in Column 2 of the Skill Task Standard (STS);  
2) A minimum of 12 months of On-the-Job Training (OJT), reduced to 6 months for retrainees;  
3) Award of Certified Alcohol and Drug Counselor (CADC) certification (SEI 475).  

Retrainees differ in that they are granted an additional 22 months to accumulate patient contact hours and professional education focused on the Twelve Core Functions/Four Domains, and they are eligible for CADC consideration only after meeting the above requirements. Note: AFRC members are not required to obtain CADC certification.
